Learning Outcomes
As a result of completing this course, students will be able to:

Frame a research question on real data using S.M.A.R.T. principles, and say what evidence would answer it;
Import, clean, and reshape messy real-world data in R;
Explore data and describe it honestly, choosing summaries and visualizations that do not mislead;
Reason with random variables: compute and interpret expectation and variance, apply conditional probability and Bayes’ rule, and explain why variances of independent quantities add;
Derive and use the fact that the standard error of a mean is \sigma/\sqrt{n}, and reason about sampling distributions and the central limit theorem by simulation rather than taking them on faith;
Construct and interpret confidence intervals, and state precisely what they do and do not claim;
Choose and carry out an appropriate hypothesis test, interpret a p-value without overstating it, compute the power of a study and say what a non-significant result is worth, and recognize when multiple comparisons have inflated a finding;
Design and analyze a randomized experiment end to end—assignment, sample size, guardrail metrics and a stopping rule fixed in advance—and explain what randomization licenses that an observational comparison does not;
Judge whether a sample can answer the question asked of it, identifying selection and survivorship bias and reasoning about why data is missing rather than only removing it;
Fit a linear model and treat each coefficient as an estimate: report its standard error and confidence interval, test it, and state precisely what “holding the other variables constant” does and does not buy;
Check a fitted model rather than trusting it—read residual plots, identify influential observations, and explain how confounding and omitted variables change a conclusion, and state what would be needed to license a causal claim without an experiment;
Distinguish a confidence interval from a prediction interval, and use resampling—the bootstrap and permutation tests—to quantify uncertainty when the standard formulas do not apply;
Fit and interpret a logistic model, report odds ratios with intervals, and explain why linear regression will not do for a binary outcome;
Collaborate with teammates using Git, and defend a shared analysis out loud to both technical and general audiences.

What AI changes, and what it does not
You are expected to use AI assistants in this course. Not tolerated—expected. You are also expected to catch them when they are wrong, and that expectation is where the grading lives.
Be clear about what has actually changed. A competent model will now write most of the code in this course faster than you can, and more idiomatically than you will by December. Asking for a function that reads a CSV, drops impossible values, and plots a distribution by group is a solved problem. Fighting that fact does not make you a better analyst; it makes you a slower one.
What has not changed is everything the previous six sections were about. A model will happily:

answer the question you typed rather than the one you meant;
compute a mean over a factor’s integer codes and report it without comment;
pool over a subgroup that reverses the conclusion, because nothing in your prompt mentioned severity;
drop the missing values, because na.rm = TRUE makes the error go away;
write “X increases Y” in the summary paragraph of an analysis that established a correlation;
and produce all of the above in clean, well-commented, confident-sounding code.

Every one of those failures produces output that runs. That is the point. The failure mode of the modern toolchain is not the error message; it is the plausible number. Detecting a plausible number requires knowing what the right answer should look like—which is judgment, and judgment is what this course is for.
Hence the AI policy in the syllabus, which is worth re-reading in this light. On homework you submit the prompt log and a critique: what did the assistant get wrong, or what would have gone wrong had you accepted its output unchanged? A submission with correct code and a thoughtless critique scores below one with imperfect code and a sharp critique. That is not a quirk of the grading. It is the grading tracking the skill.
In-class work is unaided, but “unaided” does not mean “from memory”. You may read the R documentation and these notes freely during labs and exams. Function names are meant to be looked up; nobody in professional practice recalls the argument order of merge. Reasoning is not something you look up.

Could a competent stranger, given your repository and nothing else, reproduce every number in your report?




an object that was created in the console and never written into a script;
an absolute file path that names one person’s home directory;
a data file that was edited by hand in Excel, so the transformation exists nowhere;
a random draw with no seed, so the numbers move every time.








Reproducibility is not correctness





A reproducible analysis can be entirely wrong. Reproducibility means the result follows from the data by a path anybody can walk—it says nothing about whether that path was a good one.
But it is the precondition for finding out. An irreproducible wrong answer cannot be diagnosed by anyone, including its author. This is why the two skills belong in the same course: judgment about evidence is worthless if nobody can inspect what you did.
